# Put rotational block devices on BFQ so cgroup IOWeight does something
# (2026-09-12).
#
# IOWeight=100 has been declared on every user slice and IOWeight=200 on
# system.slice for months, and until now none of it had any effect: sda ran the
# `none` scheduler and no blk-iocost model was configured, which leaves io.weight
# inert. BFQ is the scheduler that honours cgroup weights, and it is built for
# this case, interactive latency on a rotational device.
#
# This matters more since 2026-09-12 than it did before. While guest reads were
# capped at 120 IOPS no user could saturate the disk; at 400 they can, so
# arbitration between users stops being theoretical.
#
# Cost is CPU, which this box has: measured cpu.pressure 0.61 against io 80.20.
# Scoped to rotational devices, so an SSD added later is left on `none`.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="block", KERNEL=="sd[a-z]", ATTR{queue/rotational}=="1", ATTR{queue/scheduler}="bfq"
